<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	What the Hardened Runtime is asked to allow, and nothing else.

	Notarization requires the Hardened Runtime, which switches off several
	things a normal process may do. Two of them are things Chromium cannot
	work without: V8 compiles JavaScript to machine code at runtime, and it
	needs pages that are both writable and executable to do it. Without these
	the application launches and immediately dies.

	**What is deliberately absent matters more than what is here.**

	  com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
	      Would let this process load code signed by somebody other than us.
	      Electron apps routinely carry it because they load native modules
	      from npm; this one has none — every dependency is JavaScript — so
	      the check stays on. An authenticator that will load a stranger's
	      dylib is not one worth having.

	  com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables
	      Would let the environment inject libraries at launch. Nothing here
	      needs it, and it is the oldest trick there is for getting code into
	      somebody else's process.

	  com.apple.security.cs.debugger / get-task-allow
	      Would let another process attach to this one and read its memory —
	      which, in this application, is the vault while it is unlocked.

	If a future dependency needs one of these, the honest thing is to change
	the dependency.

	This same file is used for `entitlements` and `entitlementsInherit`, so the
	helper processes get exactly what the main one gets. Two files with the
	same purpose drift, and the one that drifts is always the one nobody reads.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
	<dict>
		<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
		<true/>
		<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
</plist>
